    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d833">

                <pb facs="0917" n="631" xml:id="pg631"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 351.622a/28</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-01-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 6, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 6.20 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1194. I immediately brought substance Department’s 1295 to attention Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs, who informed me that French Government would proceed at once to
                    consult with her ally, Great Britain, regarding assurance desired, as to future
                    attitude towards alien enemies on American ships. He stated that in his judgment
                    it might be difficult to differentiate between persons actually incorporated in
                    enemies’ armed forces and those who were acting as paid agents (French
                    Government had been informed of serious plots and spying by such agents). He
                    also stated that captain of <hi rendition="#italic">Descartes</hi> had been
                    ordered to act with greatest care and circumspection and not to stop any more
                    American vessels.</p>
                <p>Minister for Foreign Affairs expressed satisfaction over the prompt release of
                    the men and concluded his remarks by saying that French Government was most
                    anxious to remove any possible cause of friction between France and United
                    States.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
